  "id": "Compound::DB00962 - Gene::2562",
  "verbalization": "Zaleplon, a sleep aid, is linked to Gabrb3 through a series of molecular and functional interactions. The evidence suggests that Zaleplon binds to Gabra1 and Gabra2, which are subunits of the Gaba receptor, a type of ionotropic glutamate receptor. These receptors are involved in the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba Receptor Activity implies a role in synaptic transmission and neurophysiological processes. The fact that Gabrb3 is a participant in this activity suggests a potential functional relationship between Zaleplon and Gabrb3, possibly through the regulation of GABAergic signaling. The varying binding sites (Gabra1, Gabra2, and Gabrg2) indicate that Zaleplon may interact with different GABA receptor subunits, which could contribute to its diverse effects. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of GABA receptors in the pharmacological action of Zaleplon, though the exact mechanism of interaction with Gabrb3 remains to be fully elucidated.",
